Claude Usage Provider Implementation Plan

Goal

Add Claude Code subscription usage monitoring to the macOS Agent Usage app with a source hierarchy that prioritizes fresh account-level data while avoiding unnecessary CLI activity:

1. Fetch live usage through Claude’s OAuth usage endpoint.
2. Use a recent passive Claude Code status-line snapshot when OAuth is unavailable.
3. Offer a user-authorized Claude CLI /usage probe when no sufficiently fresh result is available.
4. Fall back to the last-known-good cached result when every live source fails.

The provider should expose five-hour and seven-day quota windows, reset times, plan and account hints when available, source metadata, freshness, and actionable recovery guidance.

The implementation is local-only and intended for personal use. Claude credentials must remain on the device and must never be logged, copied into application caches, or transmitted anywhere except directly to Anthropic.

⸻

1. Provider behavior

Automatic refresh order

For background and normal application refreshes:

OAuth live fetch
    ↓ failure
Recent status-line snapshot
    ↓ unavailable or stale
Cached last-known-good

The app must not automatically invoke Claude /usage during background refreshes.

User-initiated refresh order

When the user explicitly selects Refresh Claude Usage:

OAuth live fetch
    ↓ failure
Recent status-line snapshot
    ↓ unavailable or stale
Ask for or apply existing permission to run /usage
    ↓ failure or declined
Cached last-known-good

When the user has previously enabled automatic CLI fallback for manual refreshes, the permission prompt does not need to appear on every refresh. The /usage probe must still run only in response to a user action.

Source precedence rules

A result from a higher-priority source replaces a lower-priority result when it is valid.

Priority:

OAuth live result
> recent status-line result
> successful /usage result
> cached last-known-good

A recent status-line result can be shown instead of invoking /usage, but the user should still be able to select Force CLI Refresh… when they explicitly want a newer reading.

